---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Start with Veasel Code"
  description="Download Veasel Code, choose a model provider in Settings, and start your first coding session."
>
  <section class="doc-page page-grid">
    <p class="kicker">FIELD GUIDE / FIRST SESSION</p>
    <h1>
      From clone to
      <br />
      <em>conversation.</em>
    </h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Download the preview build for your platform, open Veasel, and configure a
      hosted or local model from the TUI.
    </p>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>What you need</h2>
      <ul class="guide-list">
        <li>
          The Veasel Code build for your operating system from the{" "}
          <a href={`${basePath}/downloads/`}>downloads page</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          A provider credential for hosted models, or a running local model
          server such as Ollama, LM Studio, llama.cpp, LocalAI, or vLLM.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The release includes the V backend and terminal client. No V compiler or
        separate server process is needed, and the bundled terminal client runs
        without Bun. On macOS you can also run{" "}
        <code>
          brew tap veasel-labs/tap &amp;&amp; brew install --cask veasel-code
        </code>
        ; on Arch Linux, <code>yay -S veasel-code-bin</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Start Veasel and choose a model</h2>
      <pre><code>veasel</code></pre>
      <p>
        Veasel reuses a healthy server or starts one at
        <code>127.0.0.1:4097</code> before opening the TUI. Press
        <kbd>s</kbd> on the home screen or enter <code>/models</code> in a
        session and choose a provider. A known catalog endpoint is filled in;
        press <kbd>Ctrl+U</kbd> to replace it. An endpoint that still contains a
        placeholder, such as a Cloudflare account ID, asks for the URL. Type an
        API key only when the provider requires one. Veasel then requests that
        provider's model list, using the key you just typed or a key already
        saved in the environment or profile. The request does not generate text.
        If the list does not come back, enter a model ID. Choosing a model saves
        the profile. The output-token budget is already filled in; press
        <kbd>Ctrl+O</kbd> to change it. 0 keeps Veasel's 4,096-token default,
        except Meta, which leaves the field unset so Meta applies its own limit.
        A chat message can contain up to 1 MiB (1,048,576 bytes) of text. That
        limit is not Muse's advertised 1,048,576-token context window. Create a
        session by selecting a workspace directory; messages and events are
        stored locally in SQLite. To run only the local API server for another
        client, use <code>veasel serve</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Review a proposed change</h2>
      <p>
        When Veasel proposes an edit, no workspace file changes. List proposals,
        open the full diff, then choose whether to apply it:
      </p>
      <pre><code>/patches
/patch show &lt;proposal-id&gt;
/patch approve &lt;proposal-id&gt;
# or: /patch reject &lt;proposal-id&gt;</code></pre>
      <p>
        Approval checks that the file still matches the reviewed version before
        writing. Conflicting or interrupted edits are not applied again
        automatically.
      </p>
    </div>
    <aside class="callout">
      <strong>Know the current boundary.</strong> Veasel stores conversation
      history locally, but sends prompts to your configured model provider. The
      model can request bounded, read-only listing, file reads, and literal
      search within the selected workspace; requested excerpts also go to that
      provider. It can propose bounded single-file edits and shell commands.
      Open each exact diff or command review before approval. Approved shell
      commands inherit the server user's permissions and have no OS sandbox. See
      the <a href={`${basePath}/docs/security/`}>security notes</a>
      before using sensitive source code.
    </aside>
    <p class="doc-next">
      <a class="text-link" href={`${basePath}/docs/providers/`}>
        Configure another provider <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href={`${basePath}/docs/`}>
        Back to the field guide <span aria-hidden="true">↑</span>
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
